\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Volumes of four-coordinate boxes}
\date{2026-10-31}

\p{Keep the four-cycle carrier #{V=\{0,1,2,3\}}. For real
#{a_i\le b_i}, let
##{B=\{x\in\mathbb R^4:a_i<x_i\le b_i\text{ for }i=0,1,2,3\}.}
The [coordinate Lebesgue measure](fdip-002H) gives
##{\lambda_V(B)=\prod_{i=0}^3(b_i-a_i).}
Each factor is the length of its half-open interval, and the defining
rectangle law multiplies them. If an endpoint pair is equal, the box
is empty and the formula gives zero.}
\p{If every lower endpoint is zero and every upper endpoint is one,
the box has volume #{1^4=1}. For lower endpoints #{(0,0,-1,0)} and
upper endpoints #{(2,1,1,3)}, its side lengths are #{(2,1,2,3)}, so
##{\lambda_V(B)=2\cdot1\cdot2\cdot3=12.}
For #{a_i=-k} and #{b_i=k} in all four coordinates, with #{k>0},
the volume is #{(2k)^4}. These volumes are unbounded as #{k} increases, so the whole
configuration space has infinite volume. Neither the edge weights nor
a mass parameter enter these values. Reference volume is not a
probability law; the later [Gaussian density](fdip-0005) supplies a
normalized weighting.}
\p{This is the rectangle formula of \ref{fdip-002H}, from
\citet{Proposition 1.7.11, pp. 197-199, preliminary version}{tao2011measure}.
Mathlib's \leanref{MeasureTheory.Measure.pi_pi} gives the finite-product
rectangle law; \leanref{Real.volume_Ioc} gives each interval length.}
